//- 0:00 TITLE, then 0:15 COLOR DOESN'T EXIST: the thesis, said plainly. Straight in: no hello, no who-am-I. Everything after this is evidence for it. docs/TALK.md has the run.

section.cover(data-timing="15")
  p.a-talk-by a talk by #{authorHandle}
  h1= talkTitle
  aside.notes
    ul
      li Straight in. Every second before 4:00 counts toward the vote.

//- Each thesis slide is an image with a caption to talk around.

//- A beautiful photo, flatly denied. The caption is the yes path's one shout.
section.over-media.is-shout(data-background-image="img/rainbow.jpg" data-timing="15")
  h2 Color doesn't exist.
  p (light has a wavelength. that's all it has.)
  aside.notes
    ul
      li Say it flat, over the prettiest rainbow anyone here has seen. No hedging.
      li Visible light is a sliver, about 380 to 700 nanometres, between infrared and ultraviolet.

//- Real cone mosaics from eight living eyes, in false color: blue, green and red dots are S, M and L cones. Hofer et al. 2005, figure 4, shown whole because it's a figure from a paper, above a one-line caption.
section.over-media.portrait.is-figure.one-line(data-timing="20")
  img(src="img/cone-mosaics.jpg" alt="False-color maps of the cones in eight people's retinas, blue, green and red dots in very different proportions")
  h2 Your eye writes three numbers.
  cite.credit Hofer et al., Journal of Neuroscience, 2005
  aside.notes
    ul
      li These are real cones, in eight living human eyes, photographed through adaptive optics. Blue, green and red dots are the short, medium and long cones.
      li Three kinds of cone squash a whole spectrum into three numbers. Say cones, not rods and cones: rods don't see color.
      li Look how uneven they are. Same species, same eyes, normal color vision, and one person is mostly green while another is almost all red. Their L to M cone ratios run from about 1:1 to 16:1, and they all agree on yellow.
      li The blue cones are a small minority in everyone, and about the same in all eight.
      li Different spectra with the same three numbers look identical. That's how every screen in the room works.

//- Kitaoka's "red strawberry illusion" (sRGB, 2017). No pixel in it has red as its strongest channel. The credit leaves out the word "illusion" so it doesn't give the game away, and keeps his name and site address, as his terms ask.
section.over-media.portrait(data-timing="25")
  img(src="img/strawberries.jpg" alt="A strawberry tart under a strong cyan cast, where the strawberries still look red")
  h2 Your brain makes up the rest.
  cite.credit 
    a(href="https://www.ritsumei.ac.jp/") Akiyoshi Kitaoka
    | 's "Strawberry Illusion"
  aside.notes
    ul
      li The thesis. Everything after this is evidence.
      li Say it: every color you've ever named was your head pretending to be the world.
      li Say it: there isn't a single red pixel in this picture. Not one pixel has red as its strongest channel. Your brain discounts the cyan light and paints the strawberries red anyway.
      li It's Akiyoshi Kitaoka's red strawberry illusion. His credit is on the slide.
